INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Atherosclerosis & Arterial Disease','easy','mcq','Which cell type is primarily responsible for forming foam cells in atherosclerotic plaques?','["A. Neutrophils","B. Macrophages","C. T lymphocytes","D. Mast cells"]','B','Macrophages ingest oxidized LDL via scavenger receptors (SR-A, CD36), becoming lipid-laden foam cells. These are the hallmark of the fatty streak, the earliest visible atherosclerotic lesion. Smooth muscle cells can also form foam cells but macrophages predominate. The "response-to-injury" hypothesis places macrophage activation as central to atherogenesis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Atherosclerosis & Arterial Disease','easy','mcq','Which lipoprotein is most strongly associated with increased risk of atherosclerosis?','["A. HDL","B. VLDL","C. LDL","D. Chylomicrons"]','C','LDL transports cholesterol from the liver to peripheral tissues. Elevated LDL leads to subendothelial accumulation, oxidation, and foam cell formation. HDL is protective (reverse cholesterol transport). LDL receptor mutations (familial hypercholesterolaemia) cause accelerated atherosclerosis, confirming LDL''s central role.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Atherosclerosis & Arterial Disease','easy','mcq','Where do atherosclerotic plaques preferentially develop?','["A. Venules","B. Capillaries","C. Branch points and vessel ostia","D. Pulmonary veins"]','C','Plaques occur preferentially at arterial branch points, curvatures, and ostia where disturbed (turbulent) flow patterns exist. Laminar flow induces atheroprotective genes (e.g., superoxide dismutase) in endothelium, whereas oscillatory shear stress promotes endothelial dysfunction and lipoprotein accumulation. Large elastic and medium muscular arteries are the primary targets.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Atherosclerosis & Arterial Disease','medium','mcq','A 55-year-old man with poorly controlled diabetes and hypertension undergoes coronary angiography. A plaque with a thin fibrous cap and a large lipid core is identified. Which feature makes this plaque most likely to cause an acute MI?','["A. Heavy calcification of the cap","B. Large fibrous cap with smooth muscle hyperplasia","C. Thin fibrous cap susceptible to rupture","D. Absent inflammatory infiltrate"]','C','Vulnerable (unstable) plaques have thin fibrous caps, large lipid cores, and abundant macrophage/foam cell infiltration at the shoulder regions. Macrophages release MMPs that degrade the collagen of the fibrous cap. Plaque rupture exposes the thrombogenic core to blood, triggering platelet aggregation and thrombus formation — the mechanism of most acute coronary syndromes.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Atherosclerosis & Arterial Disease','medium','mcq','A 48-year-old woman presents with intermittent claudication. Duplex ultrasound reveals significant atherosclerosis of the popliteal artery. Which earliest histological lesion would have been present decades earlier?','["A. Fibrous plaque","B. Fatty streak","C. Complicated plaque","D. Mural thrombus"]','B','The fatty streak is the earliest lesion of atherosclerosis, appearing as a yellow intimal discoloration. Histologically, it consists of lipid-laden macrophages (foam cells) and occasional T lymphocytes in the intima. Fatty streaks are present in the aortas of children and do not cause symptoms. They can progress to fibrous plaques and eventually complicated lesions over decades.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Atherosclerosis & Arterial Disease','medium','mcq','A pathologist examining an atherosclerotic plaque notes a fibrous cap composed mainly of smooth muscle cells and collagen, overlying a necrotic core with cholesterol crystals and calcification. Which of the following best describes the composition of the fibrous cap?','["A. Type IV collagen secreted by endothelial cells","B. Collagen synthesized by migrated smooth muscle cells","C. Fibrin deposited from organizing thrombus","D. Elastin from medial fragmentation"]','B','The fibrous cap is composed primarily of collagen (types I and III), proteoglycans, and smooth muscle cells that have migrated from the media into the intima under the influence of PDGF and other growth factors. These smooth muscle cells assume a synthetic phenotype and secrete extracellular matrix. Cap stability depends on the balance between collagen synthesis (SMCs) and degradation (MMPs from macrophages).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Atherosclerosis & Arterial Disease','medium','mcq','A 62-year-old smoker has a carotid endarterectomy. The resected specimen shows a plaque with overlying thrombus, plaque rupture, and intraplaque hemorrhage. Which protein released from activated macrophages most directly destabilizes the fibrous cap?','["A. Tissue plasminogen activator","B. Matrix metalloproteinases","C. Prostacyclin","D. Nitric oxide"]','B','Macrophages within atherosclerotic plaques secrete matrix metalloproteinases (collagenases, gelatinases, stromelysins) that degrade collagen in the fibrous cap, reducing mechanical integrity. Reduced SMC numbers (due to apoptosis triggered by inflammatory mediators) also impair collagen synthesis. This imbalance between MMP activity and TIMP inhibition leads to cap thinning and vulnerability to rupture.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Atherosclerosis & Arterial Disease','hard','mcq','A research study demonstrates that endothelial cells overlying atherosclerotic plaques show increased expression of ICAM-1 and VCAM-1. Which upstream mediator is most responsible for upregulating these adhesion molecules in early atherogenesis?','["A. Nitric oxide","B. Oxidized LDL","C. Prostacyclin (PGI2)","D. Tissue plasminogen activator"]','B','Oxidized LDL (ox-LDL) is a potent inducer of endothelial dysfunction. It upregulates VCAM-1 and ICAM-1 on endothelial cells, facilitating monocyte and T-cell adhesion and transmigration. Ox-LDL also reduces NO production (impairing vasodilation) and stimulates endothelial expression of MCP-1, promoting monocyte chemotaxis. In contrast, native LDL has minimal direct effects on endothelium.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Atherosclerosis & Arterial Disease','hard','mcq','A 45-year-old woman with a family history of premature coronary artery disease is found to have markedly elevated LDL despite a low-fat diet. Genetic testing reveals a loss-of-function mutation in the LDL receptor gene. Which mechanism directly drives foam cell formation in this patient?','["A. Excess HDL-mediated reverse cholesterol transport","B. Accumulation of LDL in the subintimal space with subsequent oxidation and uptake by macrophages via scavenger receptors","C. Direct cytotoxic effect of native LDL on endothelial cells","D. Increased secretion of VLDL from the liver"]','B','In familial hypercholesterolaemia, defective LDL receptors lead to elevated circulating LDL, which accumulates in the arterial intima. Subintimal LDL undergoes oxidative modification to ox-LDL, which is recognized by macrophage scavenger receptors (SR-A, CD36) — unlike native LDL, uptake via these receptors is not downregulated by intracellular cholesterol accumulation. This drives uncontrolled foam cell formation and accelerated atherogenesis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Atherosclerosis & Arterial Disease','medium','mcq','A 70-year-old man is found to have an incidental 6 cm infrarenal aortic aneurysm on CT scan. He is a long-term smoker. Which pathological process most directly weakens the aortic wall to permit aneurysm formation?','["A. Intimal hyperplasia obstructing vasa vasorum","B. Atherosclerosis causing medial ischemia and elastin degradation","C. Fibrinoid necrosis of adventitial vessels","D. Amyloid deposition in the media"]','B','Atherosclerosis is the major cause of abdominal aortic aneurysms. Severe atherosclerotic plaque causes ischaemia of the aortic media (by obstructing vasa vasorum), combined with local macrophage-derived MMP activity that degrades elastin and collagen. This weakens the load-bearing media, allowing progressive dilation. AAAs most commonly form infrarenally, are saccular or fusiform, and may contain laminated mural thrombus.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Ischemic Heart Disease (Angina, MI, Sudden Cardiac Death)','easy','mcq','A 60-year-old man presents with crushing chest pain radiating to the left arm that started 2 hours ago. Troponin I is markedly elevated. ECG shows ST elevation in leads V1-V4. Which zone of myocardium is at greatest risk of irreversible injury in the earliest phase?','["A. Epicardium","B. Mid-myocardium","C. Subendocardium","D. Pericardium"]','C','The subendocardium is the zone most vulnerable to ischaemia because: (1) it is the farthest from epicardial coronary arteries, (2) intramyocardial pressure is highest in this zone (compressing intramural vessels), and (3) it has the highest oxygen demand. In total coronary occlusion, the wavefront of necrosis begins in the subendocardium and progresses toward the epicardium over 3–6 hours (wavefront phenomenon).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Ischemic Heart Disease (Angina, MI, Sudden Cardiac Death)','easy','mcq','Which histological change is the earliest microscopic finding in an acute myocardial infarction, appearing within 4–12 hours of onset?','["A. Coagulation necrosis with ghost outlines","B. Wavy fibre change and early coagulative necrosis","C. Granulation tissue ingrowth","D. Dense fibrous scar formation"]','B','Within 1–4 hours, ischaemic myocardium shows wavy fibre change (stretching/undulation of myocytes due to contraction by adjacent viable muscle). By 4–12 hours, early coagulative necrosis is evident — myocytes show nuclear changes (pyknosis, karyolysis) and cytoplasmic hypereosinophilia. Macroscopically, the area may appear pale or mottled. Neutrophilic infiltration begins at 12–24 hours.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Ischemic Heart Disease (Angina, MI, Sudden Cardiac Death)','easy','mcq','A 52-year-old woman has recurrent chest pain on exertion that is relieved by rest and sublingual nitrates. Coronary angiography shows a 60% stenosis of the LAD. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["A. Unstable angina","B. Stable (classic) angina pectoris","C. Variant (Prinzmetal) angina","D. Non-ST elevation MI"]','B','Stable angina results from fixed atheromatous stenosis (>70% usually required for effort-induced symptoms) that limits coronary flow reserve. Symptoms occur predictably with exertion or emotional stress and are relieved by rest or nitrates. The underlying pathology is a stable fibrous plaque without rupture. Unstable angina involves plaque rupture with non-occlusive thrombus; Prinzmetal angina involves coronary vasospasm.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Ischemic Heart Disease (Angina, MI, Sudden Cardiac Death)','medium','mcq','A 58-year-old man who had an anterior MI 5 weeks ago presents with fever, chest pain worsened by lying flat, and a pericardial friction rub. ECG shows diffuse ST elevation. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["A. Reinfarction","B. Dressler syndrome (post-MI syndrome)","C. Acute pericarditis due to viral infection","D. Ventricular free wall rupture"]','B','Dressler syndrome (post-MI pericarditis) occurs 2–10 weeks after MI and is mediated by autoimmune mechanisms — antibodies against damaged cardiac antigens. Features include fever, pleuritic/pericardial chest pain, pericardial and pleural effusions, and elevated ESR. It must be distinguished from early post-infarction pericarditis (fibrinous, occurring 1–3 days post-MI from epicardial inflammation directly overlying the infarct).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Ischemic Heart Disease (Angina, MI, Sudden Cardiac Death)','medium','mcq','A patient dies 5 days after an acute MI. Autopsy shows a zone of yellow-white softening in the left ventricular wall with prominent neutrophilic infiltrate and early granulation tissue at the margins. What is the risk of ventricular free wall rupture at this time?','["A. Highest in the first hour","B. Peak at 3–7 days post-infarction","C. Greatest after 3 weeks","D. Absent once fibrosis begins"]','B','Myocardial rupture is most common 3–7 days post-MI when the infarcted tissue is maximally softened by enzymatic degradation (neutrophils and macrophages). At this time the necrotic myocardium has been liquefied but replacement fibrosis has not yet occurred. Free wall rupture causes haemopericardium and cardiac tamponade. Papillary muscle rupture (causing acute mitral regurgitation) and VSD also peak in this window.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Ischemic Heart Disease (Angina, MI, Sudden Cardiac Death)','medium','mcq','A 45-year-old man with no prior cardiac history collapses and dies while jogging. Autopsy reveals severe three-vessel coronary artery disease with no acute occlusion or MI. What is the most likely mechanism of death?','["A. Acute pulmonary embolism","B. Ventricular fibrillation from ischaemia-induced electrical instability","C. Complete heart block due to AV node infarction","D. Cardiac tamponade from pericardial effusion"]','B','Sudden cardiac death (SCD) most often results from ventricular fibrillation triggered by electrical instability of ischaemic myocardium. Importantly, 80–90% of resuscitated SCD survivors show no enzymatic or ECG evidence of MI — the arrhythmia arises from ischaemic (but not yet necrotic) myocardium. In 80–90% of cases, severe obstructive coronary artery disease is the underlying substrate. The triggering mechanism usually involves acute plaque change or physiological stress.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Ischemic Heart Disease (Angina, MI, Sudden Cardiac Death)','medium','mcq','Autopsy of a 65-year-old man who died 10 days after an anterior MI shows a left ventricular aneurysm filled with laminated thrombus. Which coronary artery was most likely occluded?','["A. Right coronary artery (RCA)","B. Left circumflex artery","C. Left anterior descending artery (LAD)","D. Posterior descending artery"]','C','The LAD supplies the anterior wall and apex of the left ventricle — the most common site for aneurysm formation after MI. When a large territory of myocardium is replaced by scar, the thin fibrotic wall bulges paradoxically during systole, forming an aneurysm. Mural thrombus forms within the aneurysm due to stasis and is a source of systemic emboli. RCA occlusion typically causes inferior MI.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Ischemic Heart Disease (Angina, MI, Sudden Cardiac Death)','hard','mcq','A 50-year-old woman presents with acute chest pain. ECG shows ST depression in the inferior leads. Coronary angiography reveals no significant stenosis. Ergonovine provocation causes severe coronary spasm. What is the pathophysiological mechanism of her ischaemia?','["A. Rupture of a vulnerable plaque with occlusive thrombus","B. Coronary artery vasospasm causing transmural ischaemia","C. Microvascular disease with preserved epicardial flow","D. Dynamic obstruction from papillary muscle dysfunction"]','B','Variant (Prinzmetal) angina results from episodic coronary artery vasospasm, typically at sites of atherosclerotic plaques but occasionally in angiographically normal vessels. The mechanism involves endothelial dysfunction with impaired NO release and increased sensitivity to vasoconstrictors (endothelin, thromboxane A2). Unlike stable angina, it occurs at rest and can cause ST elevation. Ergonovine (an ergot alkaloid) provokes spasm diagnostically.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Ischemic Heart Disease (Angina, MI, Sudden Cardiac Death)','hard','mcq','A pathologist stains a section of myocardium from a patient who died 18 hours after onset of chest pain. The stain used is triphenyltetrazolium chloride (TTC). What would be expected in the infarcted zone?','["A. Brick-red staining indicating viable myocardium","B. Pale/white area indicating absence of dehydrogenase activity","C. Blue staining from haematoxylin uptake by pyknotic nuclei","D. Green birefringence under polarised light"]','B','TTC is a macroscopic viability stain that reacts with dehydrogenase enzymes in viable myocardium to form a brick-red formazan precipitate. Infarcted myocardium lacks these enzymes (released into bloodstream — hence elevated troponin/CK-MB) and remains pale/white. This is the basis for identifying areas of infarction grossly at autopsy when the infarct is less than 24–48 hours old (before it becomes visibly pale/yellow macroscopically).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Heart Failure (systolic vs diastolic, compensatory mechanisms)','easy','mcq','A 68-year-old man with longstanding hypertension presents with exertional dyspnoea, orthopnoea, and bilateral basal crackles. CXR shows cardiomegaly and pulmonary congestion. What is the most likely cause of his pulmonary oedema?','["A. Right ventricular failure causing venous hypertension","B. Left ventricular failure causing elevated pulmonary venous pressure","C. Pulmonary embolism obstructing pulmonary arterial flow","D. Mitral stenosis causing left atrial hypertension only"]','B','Left-sided heart failure causes elevated left ventricular end-diastolic pressure, transmitted to the left atrium and then the pulmonary veins. Elevated pulmonary capillary wedge pressure (>18 mmHg) causes transudation of fluid into pulmonary interstitium and alveoli, producing pulmonary oedema. Clinical signs include orthopnoea, paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnoea, and basal crackles. Haemosiderin-laden macrophages ("heart failure cells") appear in alveoli.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Heart Failure (systolic vs diastolic, compensatory mechanisms)','easy','mcq','Which compensatory mechanism in heart failure leads to myocardial hypertrophy and increased wall thickness?','["A. Activation of the Frank-Starling mechanism","B. Neurohormonal activation leading to increased afterload and catecholamine-driven hypertrophy","C. Decreased preload from natriuretic peptide release","D. Reduced cardiac output stimulating peripheral vasodilation"]','B','In response to reduced cardiac output, neurohormonal systems (renin-angiotensin-aldosterone, sympathetic nervous system) are activated. Angiotensin II and catecholamines are direct trophic stimuli for cardiomyocytes, inducing hypertrophy (increased protein synthesis, sarcomere addition). Initially adaptive (maintaining wall stress by Laplace''s law), chronic hypertrophy becomes maladaptive — increasing oxygen demand, impairing diastolic function, and promoting fibrosis and arrhythmias.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Heart Failure (systolic vs diastolic, compensatory mechanisms)','easy','mcq','A patient with heart failure has a dilated left ventricle with an ejection fraction of 25%. What type of heart failure does this represent?','["A. Diastolic heart failure (HFpEF)","B. High-output heart failure","C. Systolic heart failure (HFrEF)","D. Right-sided heart failure only"]','C','Systolic (or HFrEF — heart failure with reduced ejection fraction) is characterised by impaired ventricular contractility and reduced ejection fraction (<40%). The ventricle dilates (eccentric hypertrophy) to maintain stroke volume via the Frank-Starling mechanism, but at the cost of increased wall stress. Common causes include ischaemic cardiomyopathy, dilated cardiomyopathy, and myocarditis. It contrasts with diastolic HF (HFpEF) where EF is preserved but filling is impaired.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Heart Failure (systolic vs diastolic, compensatory mechanisms)','medium','mcq','Autopsy of a patient who died of chronic left heart failure shows brown granules within alveolar macrophages. These granules stain positive with Prussian blue. What is the pathological explanation?','["A. Aspiration of iron-rich food particles","B. Haemosiderin from erythrocyte breakdown due to chronic pulmonary congestion","C. Melanin deposition from adrenal insufficiency","D. Lipofuscin accumulation in aged macrophages"]','B','Chronic left heart failure causes sustained elevation of pulmonary venous pressure, leading to chronic pulmonary congestion. Red blood cells extravasate into alveoli; alveolar macrophages phagocytose these erythrocytes and degrade haemoglobin to haemosiderin (iron-containing pigment). These haemosiderin-laden macrophages are called "heart failure cells" and are identified by Prussian blue (Perls) stain. The lung appears brown and firm ("brown induration").','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Heart Failure (systolic vs diastolic, compensatory mechanisms)','medium','mcq','An 80-year-old woman with longstanding hypertension presents with dyspnoea. Echocardiography shows a normal ejection fraction (65%) but impaired relaxation with elevated filling pressures. BNP is elevated. What is the mechanism of her heart failure?','["A. Loss of sarcomere units from myocyte dropout","B. Impaired active relaxation and increased myocardial stiffness due to concentric hypertrophy and fibrosis","C. Mitral regurgitation causing volume overload","D. Pericardial constriction limiting diastolic filling"]','B','Diastolic heart failure (HFpEF) occurs when the ventricle is stiff and cannot relax normally, leading to elevated filling pressures despite preserved systolic function. Longstanding hypertension causes concentric LV hypertrophy (pressure overload pattern — sarcomeres added in parallel). The hypertrophied myocardium, combined with interstitial fibrosis, impairs active (energy-dependent) relaxation and increases passive stiffness. Elderly women with hypertension are the classic demographic.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Heart Failure (systolic vs diastolic, compensatory mechanisms)','medium','mcq','A patient with right heart failure develops peripheral oedema, hepatomegaly with a pulsatile liver, and ascites. A liver biopsy shows centrilobular congestion and necrosis. What is this histological pattern called?','["A. Nutmeg liver","B. Budd-Chiari pattern","C. Cirrhotic transformation","D. Haemochromatosis"]','A','Right heart failure causes systemic venous hypertension, transmitted to the hepatic veins and centrilobular sinusoids. Congestion and hypoxia cause centrilobular (zone 3) hepatocyte necrosis. The gross appearance — alternating pale periportal zones and dark centrilobular zones — resembles a nutmeg ("nutmeg liver" or cardiac cirrhosis in severe chronic cases). Long-standing right HF can progress to cardiac cirrhosis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Heart Failure (systolic vs diastolic, compensatory mechanisms)','medium','mcq','Which molecular change in cardiomyocytes represents a maladaptive feature of chronic heart failure that impairs contractile function?','["A. Re-expression of foetal gene programme with β-MHC isoform replacing α-MHC","B. Increased calcium sensitivity of troponin C","C. Upregulation of SERCA2a pump activity","D. Increased β1-adrenergic receptor density"]','A','During pathological hypertrophy, cardiomyocytes undergo foetal gene re-expression, including a switch from the fast α-myosin heavy chain (higher ATPase activity, better contractility) to the slow β-MHC isoform (lower ATPase activity, reduced force development). This, combined with downregulation of SERCA2a (impairing Ca²⁺ reuptake and relaxation) and β1-receptor downregulation, contributes to the contractile dysfunction of heart failure.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Heart Failure (systolic vs diastolic, compensatory mechanisms)','hard','mcq','A researcher studies compensatory mechanisms in heart failure. Which of the following represents the transition from adaptive to maladaptive remodelling?','["A. Initial eccentric hypertrophy increasing stroke volume via Frank-Starling mechanism","B. Sustained neurohormonal activation causing myocyte apoptosis, fibrosis, and chamber dilation beyond the optimum Starling curve","C. BNP secretion promoting natriuresis and vasodilation","D. Increased heart rate from sympathetic activation restoring cardiac output"]','B','Chronic neurohormonal activation (RAAS, catecholamines, cytokines) initially compensates for reduced cardiac output but ultimately is maladaptive. Angiotensin II and noradrenaline cause myocyte apoptosis, reactive fibrosis, beta-receptor downregulation, and pathological hypertrophy. As the ventricle dilates beyond the plateau of the Starling curve, preload reserve is exhausted, and wall stress (afterload) increases further, creating a vicious cycle. ACE inhibitors and beta-blockers interrupt this cycle.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Heart Failure (systolic vs diastolic, compensatory mechanisms)','hard','mcq','A 55-year-old man with severe heart failure has a markedly elevated BNP. What is the primary stimulus for BNP secretion and what is its main physiological effect?','["A. Hypoxia stimulates BNP from pulmonary endothelium, causing pulmonary vasodilation","B. Ventricular wall stretch stimulates BNP from cardiomyocytes, causing natriuresis and vasodilation","C. Reduced renal perfusion stimulates BNP from juxtaglomerular cells, promoting sodium retention","D. Sympathetic activation stimulates BNP from adrenal medulla, increasing heart rate"]','B','BNP (B-type natriuretic peptide) is synthesised predominantly by ventricular cardiomyocytes in response to increased wall tension/stretch (volume and pressure overload). It acts on kidneys to promote natriuresis and diuresis, and on vasculature to cause vasodilation — counter-regulatory effects opposing the RAAS and sympathetic systems. Serum BNP/NT-proBNP levels correlate with heart failure severity and prognosis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Heart Failure (systolic vs diastolic, compensatory mechanisms)','medium','mcq','A 72-year-old woman with known heart failure presents to the emergency department with acute pulmonary oedema. Her chest X-ray shows "bat-wing" perihilar opacification. Which pathophysiological mechanism directly causes alveolar flooding?','["A. Decreased plasma oncotic pressure from hypoalbuminaemia","B. Increased pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure exceeding oncotic pressure","C. Inflammatory cytokines increasing alveolar permeability","D. Lymphatic obstruction from mediastinal fibrosis"]','B','Cardiogenic pulmonary oedema results from elevated pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure (from left heart failure) that exceeds plasma oncotic pressure (~25 mmHg). Fluid transudates first into the interstitium (interstitial oedema — Kerley B lines), then into alveoli. The "bat-wing" CXR pattern reflects perihilar fluid accumulation. This is a transudate (low protein) unlike ARDS (high-protein exudate from increased permeability).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Hypertensive Heart Disease','easy','mcq','A 58-year-old man with a 20-year history of hypertension has an echocardiogram showing left ventricular hypertrophy with a wall thickness of 14 mm and preserved ejection fraction. What is the morphological pattern of ventricular hypertrophy in this case?','["A. Eccentric hypertrophy","B. Concentric hypertrophy","C. Asymmetric septal hypertrophy","D. Biventricular hypertrophy"]','B','Chronic pressure overload (hypertension, aortic stenosis) causes concentric LV hypertrophy — sarcomeres are added in parallel, increasing wall thickness while cavity size is maintained or reduced. This distinguishes it from eccentric hypertrophy (volume overload — sarcomeres added in series, cavity dilates). Concentric hypertrophy increases oxygen demand and impairs diastolic filling, predisposing to diastolic HF.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Hypertensive Heart Disease','easy','mcq','What is the minimum pathological criterion required for diagnosing systemic hypertensive heart disease?','["A. Bilateral ventricular hypertrophy and pulmonary hypertension","B. Left ventricular hypertrophy (concentric) in the absence of other cardiovascular pathology, with clinical/pathological evidence of hypertension","C. Myocardial fibrosis with preserved ejection fraction","D. Coronary artery disease with superimposed LVH"]','B','The Robbins criteria for systemic HHD are: (1) LV hypertrophy (usually concentric) in the absence of other primary cardiovascular pathology that could cause LVH (such as aortic stenosis or HCM), AND (2) a clinical history or pathological evidence of hypertension in other organs (e.g., renal arteriosclerosis, hypertensive retinopathy). LV weight >500g in men or >400g in women suggests significant hypertrophy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Hypertensive Heart Disease','easy','mcq','Hypertension causes two patterns of small vessel disease. Which of the following correctly describes hyaline arteriolosclerosis?','["A. Laminated onion-skin thickening of arteriolar walls with luminal obliteration, seen in malignant hypertension","B. Homogeneous pink acellular thickening of arteriolar walls from plasma protein insudation, seen in benign hypertension","C. Fibrinoid necrosis of arterioles with inflammatory infiltrate","D. Intimal proliferation with foam cells in small vessels"]','B','Hyaline arteriolosclerosis is the small vessel change of benign (chronic) hypertension. Plasma proteins (including immunoglobulins) insude through damaged endothelium into vessel walls, producing homogeneous eosinophilic (hyaline) thickening. This narrows arteriolar lumina and increases peripheral vascular resistance. Hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis (onion-skin pattern) occurs in malignant hypertension with severe concentric smooth muscle proliferation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Hypertensive Heart Disease','medium','mcq','A 45-year-old man with blood pressure of 230/140 mmHg presents with headache, visual disturbance, and acute renal failure. Renal biopsy shows fibrinoid necrosis of arterioles and an "onion-skin" pattern of intimal thickening. What is the most accurate diagnosis?','["A. Chronic hypertension with hyaline arteriolosclerosis","B. Malignant hypertension with hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis and fibrinoid necrosis","C. Polyarteritis nodosa affecting renal arterioles","D. Thrombotic microangiopathy from HUS"]','B','Malignant (accelerated) hypertension is defined by severe hypertension (usually >200/120 mmHg) causing acute end-organ damage. The vascular lesion is hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis — concentric "onion-skin" smooth muscle proliferation — combined with fibrinoid necrosis (from plasma protein insudation and vascular wall death). This causes acute renal failure, hypertensive encephalopathy, and papilloedema. It is a medical emergency.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Hypertensive Heart Disease','medium','mcq','A patient with longstanding COPD develops right ventricular hypertrophy without left heart disease. What is the correct term for this condition and its mechanism?','["A. Left-sided hypertensive heart disease from increased cardiac output","B. Cor pulmonale (right-sided hypertensive heart disease) from pulmonary hypertension","C. Dilated cardiomyopathy from chronic hypoxia","D. Biventricular failure from systemic hypertension"]','B','Cor pulmonale (pulmonary HHD) refers to right ventricular hypertrophy and dilation caused by pulmonary hypertension from intrinsic lung disease (COPD, pulmonary fibrosis, recurrent pulmonary emboli). Chronic hypoxia causes pulmonary vasoconstriction; combined with structural vascular changes (medial hypertrophy, intimal proliferation in pulmonary arteries), this increases right ventricular afterload and drives RV hypertrophy. In acute cor pulmonale (massive PE), RV dilation occurs without hypertrophy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Hypertensive Heart Disease','medium','mcq','Autopsy of a hypertensive patient reveals a heart weighing 620g with concentric LV hypertrophy. Which complication is most directly related to the hypertrophied myocardium itself, independent of coronary atherosclerosis?','["A. Acute MI from plaque rupture","B. Sudden cardiac death from ventricular arrhythmias secondary to myocardial fibrosis and disarray","C. Aortic dissection from increased aortic wall stress","D. Right heart failure from elevated pulmonary pressures"]','B','Hypertrophied myocardium in HHD undergoes reactive interstitial fibrosis (collagen deposition between myocytes). This creates an arrhythmogenic substrate by slowing and fragmenting electrical conduction — a mechanism independent of epicardial coronary artery disease. Ventricular arrhythmias from hypertrophied fibrotic myocardium account for a significant proportion of sudden cardiac deaths in hypertensive patients.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Hypertensive Heart Disease','hard','mcq','A researcher compares concentric vs eccentric LV hypertrophy. In concentric hypertrophy from hypertension, which biomechanical principle drives sarcomere addition in parallel rather than in series?','["A. Increased preload (volume overload) causes diastolic wall stretch, stimulating longitudinal sarcomere addition","B. Increased afterload (pressure overload) causes systolic wall stress, stimulating lateral sarcomere addition and wall thickening by the Laplace relationship","C. Reduced ejection fraction stimulates RAAS-driven myocyte elongation","D. Increased heart rate from sympathetic activation causes sarcomere duplication"]','B','According to the Laplace relationship (wall stress = pressure × radius / 2 × wall thickness), increased intracavitary pressure (afterload) elevates systolic wall stress. To normalise this stress, the ventricle increases wall thickness by adding sarcomeres in parallel (concentric hypertrophy). In contrast, volume overload increases diastolic wall stress, stimulating sarcomere addition in series (eccentric hypertrophy with cavity dilation). These distinct adaptations explain the different morphologies of pressure vs volume overload.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Hypertensive Heart Disease','hard','mcq','A 55-year-old hypertensive man develops acute aortic dissection. Histological examination of the aortic wall shows loss of elastic fibres, smooth muscle necrosis, and mucoid material accumulation. What is this condition called and how does hypertension contribute?','["A. Marfan syndrome with fibrillin-1 mutation weakening elastin","B. Cystic medial necrosis — hypertension causes chronic haemodynamic stress, accentuating medial degeneration","C. Tertiary syphilis with aortitis destroying vasa vasorum","D. Atherosclerosis causing inflammatory medial destruction"]','B','Cystic medial necrosis (medionecrosis) — mucoid extracellular matrix accumulation between smooth muscle cells with loss of elastic fibres — predisposes to aortic dissection. Hypertension is the most common underlying condition, causing repetitive haemodynamic stress that accelerates medial degeneration. Hypertension accounts for most cases of aortic dissection. While Marfan syndrome (fibrillin-1 mutation) causes classic cystic medial necrosis, most cases of hypertensive dissection show a milder degree of medial change.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Hypertensive Heart Disease','medium','mcq','Which of the following most accurately describes the Framingham Study findings regarding hypertension and cardiac hypertrophy?','["A. Only severe hypertension (>180/110 mmHg) causes LVH","B. Even mild hypertension (>140/90 mmHg) can induce LVH if sufficiently prolonged","C. LVH only develops in men with hypertension, not women","D. LVH resolves completely with antihypertensive treatment within 6 months"]','B','The Framingham Heart Study established that even mild hypertension (>140/90 mmHg), if sustained over time, can induce left ventricular hypertrophy. This is clinically important as LVH is an independent risk factor for cardiovascular mortality, arrhythmias, and heart failure — beyond the direct effects of elevated blood pressure itself. Approximately 30% of the US population has hypertension of at least this degree.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Valvular Heart Disease (stenosis vs regurgitation)','easy','mcq','A 75-year-old man presents with the triad of angina, exertional syncope, and dyspnoea. Echocardiography shows severely calcified aortic valve leaflets with reduced orifice area (0.6 cm²) and a mean gradient of 50 mmHg. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["A. Aortic regurgitation","B. Calcific aortic stenosis","C. Mitral valve prolapse","D. Hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy"]','B','Calcific aortic stenosis is the most common valvular abnormality. The classic triad of symptoms (SAD: Syncope, Angina, Dyspnoea) reflects severe obstruction. Calcification is deposited at the base/hinge points of anatomically normal or bicuspid valve leaflets. In senile (degenerative) calcific aortic stenosis, the pathogenesis involves "wear and tear" similar to atherosclerosis — hyperlipidaemia, hypertension, and inflammation accelerate the process in the 7th–9th decades.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Valvular Heart Disease (stenosis vs regurgitation)','easy','mcq','A 35-year-old woman with a history of recurrent sore throat in childhood now presents with exertional dyspnoea and haemoptysis. Echocardiogram shows a "fish-mouth" appearance of the mitral valve with commissural fusion and leaflet thickening. What is the underlying pathology?','["A. Myxomatous mitral valve degeneration","B. Rheumatic mitral stenosis from post-streptococcal inflammation","C. Infective endocarditis with vegetations","D. Carcinoid heart disease"]','B','Rheumatic fever (complication of Group A streptococcal pharyngitis via molecular mimicry) causes pancarditis. In the mitral valve, acute fibrinous vegetations along line of closure progress to leaflet thickening, commissural fusion, and chordal shortening/fusion on healing. The "fish-mouth" or "buttonhole" deformity is pathognomonic of rheumatic mitral stenosis. Aschoff bodies (nodules with Anitschkow cells — caterpillar nuclei) are the diagnostic histological lesion of acute rheumatic carditis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Valvular Heart Disease (stenosis vs regurgitation)','easy','mcq','A 28-year-old woman presents with atypical chest pain and a mid-systolic click on auscultation. Echocardiogram shows billowing of mitral valve leaflets into the left atrium during systole with leaflet thickening and elongated chordae. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["A. Rheumatic mitral stenosis","B. Mitral valve prolapse (myxomatous degeneration)","C. Papillary muscle dysfunction from ischaemia","D. Infective endocarditis"]','B','Mitral valve prolapse (MVP) is the most common cause of mitral regurgitation in developed countries. Myxomatous degeneration of the valve — accumulation of myxoid (mucoid) extracellular matrix in the spongiosa layer causing leaflet ballooning, elongated chordae, and annular dilation — allows leaflets to prolapse (billow) into the LA during systole. The mid-systolic click is from sudden tensing of chordae. Most cases are benign but severe MR, arrhythmias, and rarely SCD can occur.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Valvular Heart Disease (stenosis vs regurgitation)','medium','mcq','A 30-year-old man with a known bicuspid aortic valve (BAV) develops aortic stenosis requiring valve replacement at age 35. Microscopy of the resected valve shows calcium deposited at the hinge points with preservation of leaflet architecture. Why does BAV cause earlier stenosis than tricuspid aortic valves?','["A. BAV leaflets are intrinsically thicker and more prone to infection","B. Abnormal leaflet architecture creates turbulent transvalvular flow, causing accelerated ''wear and tear'' and earlier calcium deposition","C. BAV is always associated with aortic coarctation, causing increased afterload","D. BAV leaflets produce excess fibronectin that promotes calcification"]','B','Congenitally bicuspid aortic valves are present in ~1–2% of the population. The two unequal leaflets create eccentric, turbulent transvalvular flow with increased mechanical stress. This accelerates the same "wear-and-tear" calcification process that causes senile calcific aortic stenosis in tricuspid valves, but it manifests 1–2 decades earlier (5th–6th decade vs 7th–9th decade). BAV is also a risk factor for aortic root dilation and dissection.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Valvular Heart Disease (stenosis vs regurgitation)','medium','mcq','A pathologist examining a heart at autopsy finds small, irregular, warty vegetations along the line of closure of both the mitral and tricuspid valves, on both the atrial and ventricular surfaces. There is no valve destruction. The patient had SLE. What is this lesion?','["A. Acute rheumatic endocarditis (Aschoff nodules)","B. Libman-Sacks endocarditis (non-bacterial thrombotic endocarditis of SLE)","C. Infective endocarditis with Streptococcus viridans","D. Carcinoid plaques"]','B','Libman-Sacks endocarditis is the characteristic cardiac lesion of systemic lupus erythematosus. It consists of small (1–4 mm), sterile, warty vegetations on BOTH surfaces of valve leaflets (atrial and ventricular — distinguishing it from rheumatic disease which affects mainly the atrial surface). The lesions are due to immune complex deposition and antiphospholipid antibodies. Any valve can be affected; mitral and tricuspid are most common.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Valvular Heart Disease (stenosis vs regurgitation)','medium','mcq','A 65-year-old woman with metastatic ileal carcinoid tumour presents with right-sided heart failure. Echocardiography shows tricuspid regurgitation and pulmonary stenosis with thickened, retracted valves. What is the pathological basis of these changes?','["A. Metastatic deposits of carcinoid cells within valve leaflets","B. Serotonin and other vasoactive substances from carcinoid tumour causing fibrous plaque deposition on right-sided cardiac valves","C. Immune complex deposition from tumour antigens","D. Direct invasion of valve annulus by metastatic carcinoid"]','B','Carcinoid heart disease occurs because vasoactive substances secreted by carcinoid tumours (serotonin, bradykinin, histamine) reach the right heart before being metabolised by the lungs. These substances stimulate fibroblasts to deposit fibrous plaques on the endocardial surfaces of the right heart, particularly the tricuspid and pulmonary valves. Leaflet thickening and retraction cause tricuspid regurgitation and pulmonic stenosis. Left-sided involvement is rare (substances are inactivated in the lungs) unless there is a right-to-left shunt.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Valvular Heart Disease (stenosis vs regurgitation)','medium','mcq','A 40-year-old IV drug user develops fever, a new pansystolic murmur, Janeway lesions, and Osler nodes. Blood cultures grow Staphylococcus aureus. Echocardiogram shows large, destructive vegetations on the tricuspid valve. What distinguishes these vegetations from those of rheumatic fever?','["A. Rheumatic vegetations are larger and more destructive","B. Infective endocarditis vegetations are bulky, irregular, and destructive, containing organisms, fibrin, and inflammatory cells","C. Rheumatic vegetations occur on the tricuspid valve predominantly","D. Infective vegetations are sterile and non-destructive"]','B','In infective endocarditis, vegetations are large (can be several centimetres), irregular, and destructive — they erode and destroy valve leaflets. They consist of fibrin, platelets, necrotic debris, and organisms. In contrast, acute rheumatic endocarditis produces small (1–2 mm), bead-like, sterile vegetations along the line of closure without early valve destruction. IVDU classically causes right-sided (tricuspid) endocarditis with S. aureus.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Valvular Heart Disease (stenosis vs regurgitation)','hard','mcq','The Aschoff body is the pathognomonic histological lesion of rheumatic carditis. Which cell within the Aschoff body has the characteristic "caterpillar" (owl-eye) nuclear morphology?','["A. Multinucleate giant cells (Aschoff giant cells)","B. Anitschkow cells (activated macrophages/histiocytes with caterpillar nuclei)","C. Neutrophils with hypersegmented nuclei","D. Reed-Sternberg cells"]','B','Aschoff bodies are focal granulomatous lesions in the myocardium in acute rheumatic carditis. They consist of: (1) a central zone of fibrinoid necrosis, (2) surrounding Anitschkow cells — activated macrophages/histiocytes with characteristic elongated, ribbon-like (caterpillar) chromatin or an "owl-eye" appearance on cross-section, and (3) Aschoff giant cells (multinucleate forms of Anitschkow cells). Lymphocytes, plasma cells, and occasional neutrophils are also present.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Valvular Heart Disease (stenosis vs regurgitation)','hard','mcq','A patient with chronic severe aortic regurgitation is being monitored. What haemodynamic combination is unique to aortic regurgitation and how does the left ventricle compensate?','["A. Pressure overload alone — compensated by concentric hypertrophy","B. Combined volume AND pressure overload — compensated by eccentric hypertrophy with wall thickening (''athlete heart''-like morphology)","C. Volume overload alone — compensated by eccentric hypertrophy without wall thickening","D. Reduced preload — compensated by increased heart rate"]','B','In chronic aortic regurgitation, the LV receives both normal antegrade venous return (preload) AND the regurgitant volume from the aorta. The LV must generate enough pressure to open the aortic valve against systemic resistance PLUS eject the regurgitant volume. This creates combined volume overload (dilating the cavity — eccentric hypertrophy) and effective pressure overload (requiring wall thickening to maintain wall stress). The LV in severe chronic AR may reach enormous size ("cor bovinum").','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Valvular Heart Disease (stenosis vs regurgitation)','medium','mcq','A 50-year-old woman develops mitral stenosis 20 years after an episode of rheumatic fever. She now has atrial fibrillation. Which of the following is the most important consequence of elevated left atrial pressure over time?','["A. Left ventricular hypertrophy","B. Right ventricular failure and pulmonary hypertension","C. Aortic dilation and dissection","D. Hepatic artery stenosis"]','B','Mitral stenosis obstructs flow from LA to LV, raising left atrial pressure. This is transmitted backward to the pulmonary vasculature, causing pulmonary venous hypertension, then reactive pulmonary arterial hypertension. Chronic pulmonary hypertension causes right ventricular pressure overload, hypertrophy, and eventually right heart failure (presenting as peripheral oedema, ascites, hepatomegaly). LA dilation from MS also causes AF, increasing thromboembolic risk.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Cardiomyopathies (dilated, hypertrophic, restrictive)','easy','mcq','A 35-year-old man presents with progressive dyspnoea, fatigue, and bilateral lower limb oedema over 6 months. Echocardiogram shows a globally dilated, poorly contracting left ventricle with EF of 20%. There is no history of hypertension, valve disease, or coronary artery disease. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["A. Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","B. Dilated cardiomyopathy","C. Restrictive cardiomyopathy","D. Arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy"]','B','Dilated cardiomyopathy (DCM) is the most common cardiomyopathy (90% of cases) and presents with biventricular dilation and systolic dysfunction. The heart is flabby, pale, and globally hypocontractile. Causes include genetic mutations (cytoskeletal proteins — dystrophin, desmin, lamin A/C), viral myocarditis (Coxsackievirus B), alcohol, peripartum, and idiopathic. The heart in DCM is a "bag of water" contrasting with the hypertrophied, hypercontractile heart of HCM.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Cardiomyopathies (dilated, hypertrophic, restrictive)','easy','mcq','A 20-year-old athlete collapses and dies during a basketball game. Autopsy shows a markedly thickened interventricular septum (25 mm) with systolic anterior motion of the mitral valve and microscopic myocyte disarray. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["A. Dilated cardiomyopathy","B. Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM)","C. Arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy","D. Restrictive cardiomyopathy from amyloid"]','B','Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM) is the leading cause of sudden cardiac death in young athletes. It is caused by mutations in sarcomere proteins (most commonly beta-myosin heavy chain and cardiac troponin T). The hallmarks are: asymmetric septal hypertrophy, myocyte disarray (haphazard arrangement of myocytes at microscopy), and interstitial fibrosis. LVOTO from systolic anterior motion (SAM) of the mitral valve causes the dynamic outflow obstruction in ~1/3 of cases.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Cardiomyopathies (dilated, hypertrophic, restrictive)','easy','mcq','A 70-year-old man presents with dyspnoea, peripheral oedema, and a thick "ground glass" appearance of the myocardium on echocardiogram with diastolic dysfunction and preserved EF. Cardiac biopsy shows eosinophilic deposits between myocytes that stain with Congo red and show apple-green birefringence under polarised light. What is the diagnosis?','["A. Dilated cardiomyopathy","B. Sarcoid cardiomyopathy","C. Cardiac amyloidosis (restrictive cardiomyopathy)","D. Haemochromatosis"]','C','Cardiac amyloidosis is a classic cause of restrictive cardiomyopathy. Amyloid (misfolded protein fibrils) deposits in the interstitium between cardiomyocytes, impairing diastolic filling without initially reducing systolic function. Congo red staining under polarised light shows apple-green birefringence — pathognomonic. Senile cardiac amyloidosis (transthyretin, TTR) occurs in the elderly; systemic amyloidosis (AL type) occurs with myeloma.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Cardiomyopathies (dilated, hypertrophic, restrictive)','medium','mcq','A 30-year-old woman develops shortness of breath and cardiomegaly 2 months after delivering her first child. Echocardiogram shows global LV dilation with EF of 30%. All other causes are excluded. What is the pathogenesis of this specific form of cardiomyopathy?','["A. Viral myocarditis acquired during childbirth","B. Peripartum cardiomyopathy — possibly mediated by antiangiogenic factors including prolactin cleavage products that are cardiotoxic","C. Nutritional deficiency of thiamine causing beriberi heart","D. Autoimmune destruction of cardiomyocytes by anti-cardiac antibodies from foetal microchimerism"]','B','Peripartum cardiomyopathy (PPCM) presents in the last month of pregnancy or within 5 months postpartum. Its pathogenesis involves elevated antiangiogenic mediators (sFLT1) and cardiotoxic cleavage products of prolactin (released late in pregnancy). These impair myocardial angiogenesis and directly damage cardiomyocytes. Mouse models demonstrate that blocking prolactin secretion (bromocriptine) is protective, and this is now a therapeutic strategy in humans.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Cardiomyopathies (dilated, hypertrophic, restrictive)','medium','mcq','A 45-year-old man with HCM is found to have a mutation in the gene encoding beta-myosin heavy chain. How does this sarcomere mutation lead to hypertrophy?','["A. Defective sarcomere generates less force per unit ATP, requiring more sarcomeres (compensatory hypertrophy) to maintain cardiac output","B. The mutant protein directly activates calcineurin signalling, causing hypertrophy","C. Defective myosin fails to form thick filaments, triggering apoptosis and compensatory regeneration","D. Mutant MHC activates troponin I, causing hypercontractility and direct calcium toxicity"]','A','In HCM, sarcomere mutations (most commonly beta-MHC, MYBPC3, troponin T/I) produce dysfunctional contractile proteins that generate reduced force per unit of ATP consumed. The increased energy cost of contraction triggers compensatory hypertrophic signals (via calcineurin/NFAT, CaMKII, and other pathways). This results in inappropriate ventricular hypertrophy. Additionally, the mutant proteins themselves may directly activate hypertrophic signalling.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Cardiomyopathies (dilated, hypertrophic, restrictive)','medium','mcq','A 25-year-old man presents with palpitations and near-syncope. ECG shows an epsilon wave and T-wave inversion in right precordial leads. MRI shows fatty replacement of the right ventricular free wall. What is the diagnosis?','["A. Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy with LVOTO","B. Arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy (ARVC)","C. Brugada syndrome","D. Right ventricular infarction"]','B','ARVC (arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy/dysplasia) is a genetic cardiomyopathy characterised by progressive fibro-fatty replacement of right ventricular myocardium. Mutations in desmosomal proteins (desmoplakin, plakophilin-2, desmoglein) impair cardiomyocyte adhesion, leading to cell death and fatty/fibrous replacement. This creates an arrhythmogenic substrate (re-entrant ventricular tachyarrhythmias, often triggered by exercise). The epsilon wave is a pathognomonic ECG finding.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Cardiomyopathies (dilated, hypertrophic, restrictive)','medium','mcq','A tropical patient from Brazil presents with cardiac enlargement, biventricular failure, and complete heart block. Serology is positive for Trypanosoma cruzi. What pathological process causes the myocardial damage in chronic Chagas disease?','["A. Direct parasitic invasion and destruction of cardiomyocytes by trypomastigotes","B. Chronic immune-mediated myocarditis from T-cell responses cross-reacting with cardiac antigens","C. Amastigotes releasing cardiotoxic exotoxins similar to diphtheria","D. Eosinophilic infiltration triggered by the parasite"]','B','Chagas disease (T. cruzi) causes acute myocarditis with parasites visible in myocytes. In chronic Chagas cardiomyopathy (10–20 years later), the parasites are largely absent, but the damage continues through immune-mediated mechanisms — T cells sensitised to parasite antigens cross-react with cardiac proteins (molecular mimicry). This results in progressive dilated cardiomyopathy with a predilection for the conduction system (causing complete heart block and sudden death).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Cardiomyopathies (dilated, hypertrophic, restrictive)','hard','mcq','A patient with DCM is found to have a frameshift mutation in the dystrophin gene. How does loss of dystrophin lead to cardiomyocyte dysfunction?','["A. Dystrophin deficiency impairs calcium handling by SERCA2a","B. Dystrophin connects the actin cytoskeleton to the extracellular matrix; its loss subjects the sarcolemma to mechanical stress during contraction, causing membrane tears and calcium influx leading to myocyte death","C. Dystrophin regulates troponin C calcium sensitivity and its loss reduces contractility","D. Dystrophin anchors the mitochondria to the sarcomere; its loss causes ATP depletion"]','B','Dystrophin is part of the dystrophin-glycoprotein complex (DGC) that mechanically links intracellular actin to the extracellular matrix via dystroglycans. During cardiomyocyte contraction, dystrophin transmits and distributes mechanical forces. Without dystrophin, the sarcolemma is vulnerable to micro-tears during systole, allowing uncontrolled calcium influx, mitochondrial dysfunction, and ultimately cardiomyocyte necrosis. This mechanism underlies both Duchenne/Becker muscular dystrophy-related DCM and some idiopathic DCM cases.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Cardiomyopathies (dilated, hypertrophic, restrictive)','hard','mcq','A 60-year-old man with longstanding diabetes is found to have endomyocardial fibrosis. Biopsy shows dense fibrosis restricted to the endocardium and subendocardium of both ventricles. Which cardiomyopathy does this represent and what functional consequence does it produce?','["A. Dilated cardiomyopathy with interstitial fibrosis causing systolic dysfunction","B. Restrictive cardiomyopathy — endocardial fibrosis rigidly constrains ventricular filling, causing diastolic dysfunction with preserved systolic function","C. Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy with subendocardial fibrosis from ischaemia","D. Constrictive pericarditis with inward extension of fibrosis into myocardium"]','B','Endomyocardial fibrosis is a tropical form of restrictive cardiomyopathy (common in Africa). Dense fibrosis of the endocardium (particularly involving the apices and subvalvular regions) makes the ventricular walls rigid. This restricts diastolic filling (impaired ventricular compliance) while systolic function is relatively preserved — the haemodynamic hallmark of restrictive cardiomyopathy. Mural thrombi form within the fibrosed areas and organise, potentially worsening restriction.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Cardiomyopathies (dilated, hypertrophic, restrictive)','medium','mcq','A 40-year-old woman with known sarcoidosis develops complete heart block and ventricular tachycardia. Cardiac MRI shows patchy mid-wall late gadolinium enhancement. What is the pathological basis of the cardiac manifestations?','["A. Amyloid fibril deposition in the conduction system from chronic granulomatous inflammation","B. Sarcoid non-caseating granulomas infiltrating the myocardium and conduction system, causing local destruction, fibrosis, and arrhythmogenic substrate","C. Eosinophilic infiltration of the AV node from sarcoid-related hypereosinophilia","D. Giant cell arteritis affecting coronary arteries causing ischaemic conduction disease"]','B','Cardiac sarcoidosis occurs in ~25% of sarcoid patients (often clinically silent). Non-caseating epithelioid granulomas infiltrate the myocardium, with predilection for the conduction system (AV node, bundle of His), papillary muscles, and LV free wall. Granulomas cause local necrosis, followed by fibrosis and scarring — creating an arrhythmogenic substrate for VT/VF, complete heart block, and heart failure. Mid-wall fibrosis on LGE-MRI is characteristic.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Pericardial Disease','easy','mcq','A 25-year-old man presents 2 weeks after a viral upper respiratory tract infection with sharp pleuritic chest pain relieved by leaning forward, a pericardial friction rub, and diffuse saddle-shaped ST elevation on ECG. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["A. Acute MI with pericarditis (Dressler syndrome)","B. Acute viral pericarditis","C. Purulent pericarditis from bacterial infection","D. Constrictive pericarditis"]','B','Acute viral pericarditis is the most common cause of primary pericarditis, typically caused by Coxsackievirus B or echovirus. It presents with sharp pleuritic/positional chest pain (worse supine, better sitting forward), a pericardial friction rub (pathognomonic), and saddle-shaped ST elevation on ECG. The pericardium shows fibrinous exudate (sero-fibrinous pericarditis). Most cases are self-limiting; NSAIDs ± colchicine are first-line treatment.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Pericardial Disease','easy','mcq','A patient develops acute cardiac tamponade 3 days after a myocardial infarction. Which finding would be expected on examination?','["A. Loud S1 and S2 with a systolic murmur","B. Beck''s triad: hypotension, muffled heart sounds, and raised JVP","C. Wide pulse pressure with bounding pulses","D. Bilateral basal crackles from pulmonary oedema"]','B','Cardiac tamponade occurs when fluid accumulation in the pericardial sac (haemopericardium from free wall rupture post-MI, or rapidly accumulating effusion) compresses the cardiac chambers, preventing adequate filling. Beck''s triad: hypotension (reduced cardiac output), muffled/distant heart sounds (fluid attenuates sounds), and raised JVP (impaired venous return). Pulsus paradoxus (>10 mmHg drop in systolic BP with inspiration) is also characteristic. As little as 200–300 mL can cause tamponade if rapidly accumulating.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Pericardial Disease','easy','mcq','What is the histological appearance of fibrinous pericarditis and what macroscopic description is classically given?','["A. Dense fibrous thickening with calcium deposits — ''eggshell heart''","B. Exudate of fibrin strands between pericardial layers, producing a ''bread and butter'' appearance on gross examination","C. Haemorrhagic exudate with abundant neutrophils — ''strawberry jam'' pericardium","D. Pus filling the pericardial sac — ''purulent oyster''"]','B','Fibrinous pericarditis is characterised grossly by a shaggy, rough surface with fibrin strands connecting the parietal and visceral pericardium — classically described as "bread and butter" pericarditis (because it resembles two pieces of buttered bread pulled apart). Microscopically, fibrin strands admixed with inflammatory cells cover the serosal surfaces. It occurs in uremia, acute rheumatic fever, post-MI (within 1–3 days), and SLE.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Pericardial Disease','medium','mcq','A 55-year-old man with a history of tuberculosis presents with progressive exertional dyspnoea, peripheral oedema, and ascites. JVP is elevated and does not fall with inspiration (Kussmaul sign). Echocardiogram shows normal ventricular function but thickened, calcified pericardium with respiratory variation in ventricular filling. What is the diagnosis?','["A. Cardiac tamponade","B. Restrictive cardiomyopathy from amyloid","C. Constrictive pericarditis","D. Right-sided heart failure from cor pulmonale"]','C','Constrictive pericarditis occurs when chronic pericardial inflammation (TB is the classic cause; also idiopathic, post-radiation, post-cardiac surgery) leads to dense fibrous or fibrocalcific encasement of the heart. The rigid pericardium limits diastolic filling of all chambers equally (raised filling pressures in all chambers). Kussmaul sign (JVP rising with inspiration — opposite to normal) and respiratory variation in ventricular filling (on echo) distinguish it from tamponade (where venous pressure falls with inspiration is absent).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Pericardial Disease','medium','mcq','A patient undergoing haemodialysis for chronic kidney disease develops recurrent pericardial effusions. Pericardiocentesis yields a haemorrhagic fluid. What is the type of pericarditis and the mechanism?','["A. Suppurative pericarditis from immune suppression","B. Uraemic fibrinous pericarditis — uraemic toxins irritate the pericardium causing fibrinous and haemorrhagic exudate","C. Neoplastic pericarditis from renal cell carcinoma metastases","D. Autoimmune pericarditis from anti-GBM antibodies"]','B','Uraemic pericarditis is a direct complication of end-stage renal disease. Retained uraemic toxins (including urea and other nitrogenous metabolites) act as irritants to the pericardial serosa, causing fibrinous and often haemorrhagic pericarditis. It is a classic cause of serous-fibrinous and haemorrhagic pericarditis listed in Robbins. It typically occurs when creatinine is severely elevated and usually resolves with dialysis. The fluid is often haemorrhagic due to capillary fragility.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Pericardial Disease','medium','mcq','An immunocompromised patient post-cardiac surgery develops purulent pericarditis. Which organism most commonly causes purulent (suppurative) pericarditis?','["A. Mycobacterium tuberculosis","B. Staphylococcus aureus or other pyogenic bacteria","C. Coxsackievirus B","D. Histoplasma capsulatum"]','B','Purulent (suppurative) pericarditis results from bacterial infection, most commonly Staphylococcus aureus, Streptococcus pneumoniae, or gram-negative organisms. Routes include direct spread from pneumonia/empyema, haematogenous seeding, or postoperative infection. The pericardial sac contains frank pus with neutrophilic exudate. It is a serious condition requiring drainage and antibiotics; it can progress to constrictive pericarditis if untreated.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Pericardial Disease','medium','mcq','An autopsy reveals a "soldier''s plaque" — a focal fibrous thickening of the pericardial serosal membrane. What does this represent?','["A. Calcification of a prior haemopericardium","B. Organised fibrinous pericarditis resulting in plaque-like fibrous adhesions that rarely impair cardiac function","C. Primary pericardial mesothelioma","D. Pericardial infiltration by lymphoma"]','B','Soldier''s plaque refers to focal fibrous thickenings of the pericardial membranes — an incidental autopsy finding representing healed/organised fibrinous pericarditis. They rarely impair cardiac function. In contrast, when fibrous organisation is more extensive, mesh-like adhesions or complete fibrous obliteration of the pericardial sac can occur. If the sac is obliterated but the pericardium remains pliable, function is usually preserved. Calcification of the pericardium (''eggshell pericardium'') indicates chronic constrictive pericarditis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Pericardial Disease','hard','mcq','A patient develops adhesive mediastinopericarditis after cardiac surgery. Which distinctive clinical finding would suggest this diagnosis on physical examination?','["A. Pulsus paradoxus — exaggerated drop in systolic BP with inspiration","B. Systolic retraction of the chest wall and diaphragm (Broadbent sign) with each heartbeat","C. Displaced apex beat with parasternal heave","D. Wide fixed splitting of S2"]','B','In adhesive mediastinopericarditis, the obliterated pericardial sac adheres to surrounding mediastinal structures. With each systolic contraction, the heart pulls on the adherent structures, causing systolic retraction of the chest wall and diaphragm (Broadbent sign). This is a distinctive physical finding not seen in other forms of pericardial disease. The cardiac workload is markedly increased by having to contract against these external adhesions, eventually causing hypertrophy and dilation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Pericardial Disease','hard','mcq','A researcher studies the haemodynamics of cardiac tamponade vs constrictive pericarditis. Which statement correctly distinguishes these two conditions?','["A. Both show equalisation of diastolic pressures in all chambers; tamponade shows pulsus paradoxus, while constriction shows Kussmaul sign","B. Tamponade shows elevated venous pressure that rises with inspiration; constriction shows pulsus paradoxus","C. Constriction only limits systolic function; tamponade limits diastolic function","D. Both conditions cause pericardial calcification on imaging"]','A','Both tamponade and constrictive pericarditis elevate and equalise diastolic filling pressures in all four chambers (RVEDP = LVEDP). Key distinction: cardiac tamponade causes pulsus paradoxus (>10 mmHg BP drop with inspiration — right ventricular filling during inspiration competes with LV for limited space). Constrictive pericarditis shows Kussmaul sign (JVP paradoxically rises with inspiration — the rigid pericardium cannot transmit the normal inspiratory pressure drop to the right heart, so venous return augmentation is impaired).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Pericardial Disease','medium','mcq','A 45-year-old woman with breast cancer develops progressive dyspnoea. CXR shows a flask-shaped cardiac shadow. Echocardiography demonstrates a large circumferential pericardial effusion with early tamponade physiology. What is the most common mechanism of malignant pericardial effusion?','["A. Direct invasion of the pericardium by primary pericardial mesothelioma","B. Haematogenous metastatic deposits in the pericardium causing exudative effusion","C. Chemotherapy-induced cardiac toxicity causing pericardial inflammation","D. Radiation-induced constrictive pericarditis"]','B','Metastatic tumours to the pericardium occur in approximately 5% of persons dying of cancer (much more common than primary pericardial tumours). The most common primary sites are lung, breast, melanoma, and leukaemia/lymphoma. Pericardial metastases cause malignant pericardial effusion by obstructing lymphatic drainage and directly secreting exudative fluid. The flask-shaped cardiac silhouette on CXR indicates >250 mL of pericardial fluid.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Congenital Heart Disease (ASD, VSD, Tetralogy, TGA, PDA)','easy','mcq','A 2-year-old child is noted to have a harsh pansystolic murmur at the left sternal border. Echocardiography confirms a ventricular septal defect. The defect creates a left-to-right shunt. What determines the direction of blood flow in this shunt?','["A. Aortic pressure is lower than pulmonary artery pressure","B. Left ventricular pressure exceeds right ventricular pressure, so blood flows left to right","C. Pulmonary vascular resistance exceeds systemic vascular resistance","D. The right atrium has lower pressure than the left atrium"]','B','In a VSD with a left-to-right (L→R) shunt, blood flows from the high-pressure left ventricle to the lower-pressure right ventricle because systemic vascular resistance exceeds pulmonary vascular resistance. This causes increased pulmonary blood flow, left heart volume overload, and pulmonary congestion. VSDs are the most common congenital heart defect (25–30% of all CHD). Small VSDs may close spontaneously; large ones risk Eisenmenger syndrome if left untreated.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Congenital Heart Disease (ASD, VSD, Tetralogy, TGA, PDA)','easy','mcq','A 6-year-old girl is found to have a loud systolic murmur and a fixed split S2. Cardiac catheterisation shows higher oxygen saturation in the right ventricle than in the right atrium. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["A. Patent ductus arteriosus","B. Atrial septal defect","C. Ventricular septal defect","D. Tetralogy of Fallot"]','C','A step-up in oxygen saturation between the right atrium and right ventricle indicates a left-to-right shunt at the ventricular level, consistent with VSD. The step-up would occur at the atrial level in ASD. Fixed split S2 is characteristic of ASD (not VSD). The question features VSD as the most common CHD, demonstrating an oxygen step-up on oximetry from RA to RV during catheterisation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Congenital Heart Disease (ASD, VSD, Tetralogy, TGA, PDA)','easy','mcq','A newborn with cyanosis is found to have: pulmonary stenosis, right ventricular hypertrophy, ventricular septal defect, and overriding aorta. What is this condition and what determines the severity of cyanosis?','["A. Transposition of great arteries — severity depends on VSD size","B. Tetralogy of Fallot — severity of cyanosis depends on the degree of pulmonary stenosis","C. Truncus arteriosus — severity depends on pulmonary blood flow","D. Tricuspid atresia — severity depends on ASD size"]','B','Tetralogy of Fallot is the most common cyanotic congenital heart disease in children beyond infancy. The four components are: (1) pulmonary stenosis (RVOTO), (2) VSD, (3) overriding aorta, and (4) RV hypertrophy (compensatory). The degree of cyanosis depends on the severity of RVOTO: mild stenosis → pink Fallot (predominantly L→R); severe stenosis → right-to-left (R→L) shunt through VSD → cyanosis. The embryological basis is anterior deviation of the infundibular septum.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Congenital Heart Disease (ASD, VSD, Tetralogy, TGA, PDA)','medium','mcq','A premature neonate has a continuous "machinery" murmur at the upper left sternal border. The ductus arteriosus remains open. Indomethacin is administered. What is the mechanism of action of indomethacin in this condition?','["A. It stimulates muscular contraction of the ductus by blocking adenosine receptors","B. It inhibits prostaglandin synthesis (COX inhibition), reducing PGE2-mediated vasodilation and allowing the ductus to close","C. It increases pulmonary vascular resistance, reversing the L→R shunt","D. It blocks endothelin receptors, causing ductal constriction"]','B','The ductus arteriosus is maintained patent in foetal life by prostaglandin E2 (PGE2, from placenta/ductus wall) and relative hypoxia (which dilates the ductus). After birth, rising pO2 and falling PGE2 cause ductal constriction. In premature infants, high PGE2 levels persist. Indomethacin (a COX inhibitor) reduces PGE2 synthesis, promoting ductal closure. Prostaglandin E1 infusion is used conversely to keep the PDA open in ductal-dependent lesions (e.g., pulmonary atresia, coarctation).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Congenital Heart Disease (ASD, VSD, Tetralogy, TGA, PDA)','medium','mcq','A child with Down syndrome is found to have an endocardial cushion defect (atrioventricular septal defect). What embryological structure fails to form normally?','["A. The muscular ventricular septum from the cardiac apex","B. The endocardial cushions, which normally fuse to form the AV valves and portions of the atrial and ventricular septa","C. The infundibular (conal) septum dividing the outflow tract","D. The foramen ovale, which fails to close after birth"]','B','Endocardial cushions are specialised swellings of the AV canal that normally fuse to form: the inferior portion of the atrial septum (septum primum), the superior portion of the ventricular septum (inlet portion), and the mitral and tricuspid valve leaflets. Failure of cushion fusion causes an AV septal defect (AVSD/AVCD) — a single common AV valve orifice, primum ASD, and inlet VSD. AVSD has a strong association with Down syndrome (trisomy 21).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Congenital Heart Disease (ASD, VSD, Tetralogy, TGA, PDA)','medium','mcq','A newborn presents with profound cyanosis at birth that does not improve with oxygen administration. Echocardiogram shows the aorta arising from the right ventricle and the pulmonary artery from the left ventricle. Which structure must be present or created to allow survival?','["A. A large VSD to allow bidirectional mixing","B. Any communication between the systemic and pulmonary circulations (PDA, ASD, or VSD) to allow oxygenated and deoxygenated blood to mix","C. An intact ventricular septum to prevent Eisenmenger syndrome","D. An overriding aorta to receive oxygenated blood from both ventricles"]','B','In transposition of the great arteries (TGA), the aorta arises from the right ventricle (carrying deoxygenated blood) and the pulmonary artery from the left ventricle (carrying oxygenated blood). The systemic and pulmonary circulations run in parallel rather than in series — incompatible with life without mixing. Survival requires a communication (PDA, ASD, VSD or a patent foramen ovale) for mixing. Prostaglandin E1 is given to maintain PDA patency until balloon atrial septostomy (Rashkind) or surgical correction (arterial switch operation) is performed.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Congenital Heart Disease (ASD, VSD, Tetralogy, TGA, PDA)','medium','mcq','A 35-year-old woman is found to have an atrial septal defect. She has not been surgically corrected. What is the most feared long-term complication if untreated?','["A. Ventricular hypertrophy causing outflow obstruction","B. Eisenmenger syndrome — pulmonary hypertension reverses the shunt to R→L, causing cyanosis","C. Complete heart block from atrial enlargement","D. Aortic dissection from left atrial hypertension"]','B','Uncorrected L→R shunts (ASD, VSD, PDA) cause chronic volume overload of the pulmonary circulation. Over years, reactive pulmonary vascular disease develops (medial hypertrophy, intimal fibrosis of pulmonary arteries), eventually causing severe pulmonary hypertension. When pulmonary vascular resistance exceeds systemic resistance, the shunt reverses to R→L (Eisenmenger syndrome), causing cyanosis, polycythaemia, and digital clubbing. Once established, Eisenmenger syndrome is largely irreversible.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Congenital Heart Disease (ASD, VSD, Tetralogy, TGA, PDA)','hard','mcq','A 3-year-old child with Tetralogy of Fallot has a "tet spell" — sudden severe hypoxia with loss of consciousness. Which mechanism triggers this event?','["A. Paradoxical embolism through the VSD causing cerebral ischaemia","B. Acute infundibular spasm causing sudden worsening of RVOTO, increasing R→L shunt through VSD and reducing pulmonary blood flow","C. Sudden plaque rupture in the right coronary artery","D. Sinus node arrest from hypoxia causing cardiac output to fall"]','B','Tet spells (hypercyanotic spells) occur when acute infundibular (subpulmonary) muscle spasm acutely worsens the degree of RVOTO. This increases resistance to right ventricular outflow, forcing more blood through the R→L shunt at the VSD into the aorta. Pulmonary blood flow falls sharply, systemic hypoxaemia worsens, and acidosis further stimulates infundibular spasm (a vicious cycle). Treatment includes squatting/knee-chest position (increases SVR), oxygen, morphine, beta-blockers, and volume.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Congenital Heart Disease (ASD, VSD, Tetralogy, TGA, PDA)','hard','mcq','A genetic study of congenital heart disease reveals that mutations in the NKX2.5 transcription factor are associated with ASD and conduction abnormalities. Which aspect of cardiac development does NKX2.5 primarily regulate?','["A. Smooth muscle cell differentiation in the pulmonary vasculature","B. Cardiomyocyte differentiation, specification, and atrial septal morphogenesis from early cardiac progenitor cells","C. Neural crest cell migration into the outflow tract","D. Endocardial-to-mesenchymal transition in valve formation"]','B','NKX2.5 (cardiac homeobox transcription factor) is expressed in cardiac progenitor cells from the earliest stages of heart development and is essential for cardiomyocyte specification, differentiation, and atrial septal morphogenesis. Heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in NKX2.5 in humans cause atrial septal defects and progressive AV conduction abnormalities (AV block). NKX2.5 interacts with GATA4, TBX5, and other transcription factors in a regulatory network controlling cardiac development.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Congenital Heart Disease (ASD, VSD, Tetralogy, TGA, PDA)','medium','mcq','A child is found to have upper extremity hypertension, reduced femoral pulses, and rib notching on CXR. What is the diagnosis and what is the mechanism of rib notching?','["A. Aortic stenosis — rib notching from turbulent flow across a bicuspid valve","B. Coarctation of the aorta — rib notching from erosion by enlarged collateral intercostal arteries bypassing the coarctation","C. Pulmonary stenosis — rib notching from right ventricular strain","D. Tetralogy of Fallot — rib notching from ventricular hypertrophy"]','B','Coarctation of the aorta is a localised aortic narrowing, typically at the aortic isthmus (near the ligamentum arteriosum). Upper extremity hypertension and lower extremity hypoperfusion/reduced pulses are classic. Collateral circulation develops through intercostal arteries (enlarging to bypass the obstruction), whose pulsatile flow erodes the inferior surfaces of ribs 3–9, producing the characteristic rib notching on CXR. Associated with bicuspid aortic valve (50%), Berry aneurysms, and Turner syndrome.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Cardiac Tumours & Thrombi','easy','mcq','A 45-year-old woman develops episodes of syncope, dyspnoea, and a "tumour plop" sound on auscultation that changes with position. Echocardiography shows a pedunculated mass in the left atrium attached to the interatrial septum. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["A. Left atrial thrombus from atrial fibrillation","B. Cardiac myxoma","C. Papillary fibroelastoma of the mitral valve","D. Metastatic melanoma to the heart"]','B','Cardiac myxoma is the most common primary tumour of the adult heart. About 90% occur in the atria (left-to-right ratio ~4:1). They arise from the fossa ovalis region of the interatrial septum. Myxomas are gelatinous, polypoid tumours attached by a stalk. Clinical features are caused by: obstruction to mitral valve (tumour plop, positional symptoms), systemic embolisation (tumour fragments), and constitutional symptoms (fever, weight loss from IL-6). Surgical excision is curative.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Cardiac Tumours & Thrombi','easy','mcq','A 6-month-old infant with tuberous sclerosis is found to have multiple echogenic intracardiac masses. Most resolve spontaneously by adolescence. What is the most likely tumour?','["A. Myxoma","B. Rhabdomyoma","C. Fibroma","D. Lipoma"]','B','Rhabdomyoma is the most common cardiac tumour in infants and children. It is strongly associated with tuberous sclerosis (TSC1/TSC2 mutations — 50% of patients with tuberous sclerosis have rhabdomyomas). These hamartomas arise from ventricular myocardium and appear as multiple grey-white nodules. Histologically, "spider cells" — large glycogen-laden cells with cytoplasmic processes radiating from a central nucleus — are pathognomonic. Most regress spontaneously with age.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Cardiac Tumours & Thrombi','easy','mcq','A 65-year-old man with atrial fibrillation has a stroke. Echocardiogram reveals a large thrombus in the left atrial appendage. Which haemodynamic factors promote thrombus formation in this location?','["A. High-velocity flow and turbulence in the left atrial appendage","B. Stasis from loss of effective atrial contraction in atrial fibrillation","C. Endothelial injury from valvular calcification","D. Hypercoagulable state from protein C deficiency"]','B','Left atrial appendage (LAA) thrombus is a major complication of atrial fibrillation. In AF, the atria quiver rather than contract — the LAA has extremely low-velocity blood flow (Virchow''s triad: stasis). The narrow-necked, trabeculated LAA anatomy further promotes stasis and thrombus formation. LAA thrombi are the source of approximately 90% of cardioembolic strokes in AF patients. Anticoagulation (warfarin, DOACs) or LAA closure devices are used for prevention.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Cardiac Tumours & Thrombi','medium','mcq','A 55-year-old woman with a myxoma undergoes surgery. The resected specimen shows a gelatinous, polypoid mass with a smooth surface, attached by a stalk to the fossa ovalis. Histologically, the tumour cells are embedded in abundant myxoid stroma. Which genetic pathway is associated with familial cardiac myxoma syndromes?','["A. Mutations in RB1 tumour suppressor gene","B. Activating mutations in GNAS1 (G protein subunit) or loss-of-function mutations in PRKAR1A (Carney complex)","C. TP53 mutations causing malignant transformation","D. BCR-ABL translocation"]','B','Sporadic myxomas lack consistent genetic alterations. However, familial myxoma syndromes include: (1) Carney complex — loss-of-function mutations in PRKAR1A (regulatory subunit of cAMP-dependent protein kinase), causing myxomas at multiple sites (heart, skin, breast), skin pigmentation (lentigines, blue naevi), and endocrine tumours, and (2) McCune-Albright syndrome — activating GNAS1 mutations (Gsα subunit). Carney complex patients develop myxomas at younger ages and have higher recurrence rates.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Cardiac Tumours & Thrombi','medium','mcq','A patient with a large anterior MI develops a mural thrombus in the left ventricle over the area of dyskinetic myocardium. What is the mechanism of thrombus formation in this setting?','["A. Paradoxical embolism through a patent foramen ovale","B. Virchow''s triad: stasis over akinetic/dyskinetic myocardium, endocardial injury from the infarction, and hypercoagulability from the acute inflammatory state","C. Low platelet count from DIC complicating the MI","D. Ventricular thrombus from direct bacterial colonisation of necrotic endocardium"]','B','Post-MI mural thrombi result from all three components of Virchow''s triad: (1) Stasis — over akinetic/dyskinetic segments of the infarcted myocardium where blood swirls without being ejected; (2) Endocardial injury — necrotic myocardium and overlying endocardial damage expose subendothelial collagen and tissue factor; (3) Hypercoagulability — acute MI activates the coagulation cascade. Mural thrombi form particularly in large anterior MIs with apical dyskinesia and are a source of systemic emboli (stroke, visceral/limb ischaemia).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Cardiac Tumours & Thrombi','medium','mcq','A 55-year-old man with lung cancer is found to have multiple cardiac masses on echocardiography involving the pericardium and right ventricle. Biopsy confirms metastatic adenocarcinoma. By what route do most cardiac metastases reach the heart?','["A. Direct extension through the thoracic wall","B. Haematogenous spread or direct extension via the superior/inferior vena cava, lymphatic spread, and contiguous pericardial spread","C. Retrograde flow through the coronary arteries","D. Lymphatic spread exclusively through mediastinal nodes"]','B','Cardiac metastases occur in approximately 5% of cancer patients and are far more common than primary cardiac tumours. The most common primaries are lung, breast, melanoma, lymphoma/leukaemia, and renal cell carcinoma. Routes of spread include: haematogenous (most common for most tumours); lymphatic; direct extension (lung, oesophageal cancers, thymic tumours); and intracavitary extension via IVC (renal cell carcinoma extending as a tumour thrombus into the right heart). Pericardium is the most commonly affected cardiac structure.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Cardiac Tumours & Thrombi','medium','mcq','Non-bacterial thrombotic endocarditis (NBTE) is found at autopsy in a patient with pancreatic adenocarcinoma. What are the characteristic features of these vegetations?','["A. Large, destructive vegetations containing tumour cells on all four valves","B. Small, sterile, flat or slightly raised vegetations along the lines of valve closure, consisting of bland thrombus without organisms or inflammation","C. Warty vegetations on both surfaces of the mitral valve","D. Calcified vegetations fused to leaflets with commissural scarring"]','B','Non-bacterial thrombotic endocarditis (NBTE), also called marantic endocarditis, consists of small, sterile, bland thrombi deposited along the line of valve closure on any heart valve. They consist of bland fibrin-platelet thrombus without organisms or significant inflammatory infiltrate. NBTE is associated with debilitating states (malignancy — especially mucin-secreting adenocarcinomas — sepsis, burns, renal failure) and chronic hypercoagulable states. Despite their small size, they can embolise and cause strokes.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Cardiac Tumours & Thrombi','hard','mcq','A 30-year-old woman with recurrent systemic emboli is found to have a papillary fibroelastoma on her aortic valve. What is the characteristic histological appearance of this tumour?','["A. Spindle cells embedded in myxoid stroma with scattered lipid droplets","B. Avascular frond-like papillae covered by endothelium with a myxoid core containing elastic fibres","C. Dense fibrous tissue with focal calcification","D. Large glycogen-laden spider cells in a fibromuscular stroma"]','B','Papillary fibroelastomas are the second most common primary cardiac tumour. They are small (usually <1 cm), papillary, anemone-like tumours found on cardiac valves (especially the aortic valve). Histologically, each papilla consists of an avascular myxoid core rich in elastic fibres (explaining the ''fibroelastoma'' name), covered by a single layer of endothelium. Although benign, they cause significant morbidity by embolising from their papillary fronds.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Cardiac Tumours & Thrombi','hard','mcq','An angiosarcoma is found originating from the right atrium. Which of the following correctly describes the typical features of this malignant cardiac tumour?','["A. Most common primary cardiac malignancy; usually arises in the right atrium; locally invasive with early haematogenous metastases","B. Most common primary cardiac malignancy arising from left ventricular myocardium with lymph node spread","C. Benign tumour of vascular endothelium that spontaneously regresses in adults","D. Associated exclusively with HHV-8 infection and AIDS"]','A','Angiosarcoma is the most common primary malignant tumour of the heart. It arises from the right atrium in most cases (in contrast to myxomas, which are left atrial). Angiosarcomas are locally invasive, destroying adjacent structures and metastasising haematogenously (lungs, liver, brain). Histologically, they consist of atypical endothelial cells forming irregular vascular channels with cytological atypia (distinguished from benign haemangiomas by irregular vessels, cytological atypia, and mitoses). Prognosis is very poor.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Cardiac Tumours & Thrombi','medium','mcq','A 60-year-old man with worsening heart failure is found to have a cardiac fibroma at autopsy. What is the characteristic location and gross appearance of this tumour?','["A. Left atrial appendage; gelatinous and pedunculated","B. Ventricular myocardium (especially left ventricle/septum); firm, white, non-encapsulated mass of fibroblasts and collagen","C. Tricuspid valve leaflet; papillary fronds with myxoid core","D. Epicardial surface; lipid-yellow lobulated mass"]','B','Cardiac fibromas are benign tumours, the second most common cardiac tumour in children (after rhabdomyoma). They arise from the ventricular myocardium (typically LV free wall or septum) as firm, white, non-encapsulated masses composed of fibroblasts and dense collagen. They can be bulky, cause arrhythmias by interrupting conduction pathways, and impair cardiac function. They do not regress spontaneously (unlike rhabdomyomas) and may require surgical resection.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Aortic Diseases (aneurysm, dissection)','easy','mcq','A 70-year-old man is found incidentally to have a 5.5 cm fusiform infrarenal aortic aneurysm on CT scan. He is a heavy smoker with known hypertension. Surgery is recommended. What is the most feared acute complication of an untreated large AAA?','["A. Aortic stenosis from intraluminal thrombus","B. Rupture with catastrophic haemorrhage into the retroperitoneum or peritoneum","C. Distal embolisation causing blue toe syndrome only","D. Compression of the renal arteries causing hypertension"]','B','Rupture is the most serious complication of AAA, with a mortality rate of >80%. Risk of rupture increases exponentially with diameter: <4 cm ~0%; 4–5 cm ~1%/year; >5.5 cm ~25%/year. Rupture typically occurs into the retroperitoneum (causing flank pain, hypotension, pulsatile mass — the classic triad) or free peritoneal cavity (immediate exsanguination). Most AAAs are infrarenal, located between the renal arteries and aortic bifurcation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Aortic Diseases (aneurysm, dissection)','easy','mcq','A 35-year-old man with Marfan syndrome (FBN1 mutation) develops an aortic dissection. What is the underlying pathological change in the aortic wall in Marfan syndrome that predisposes to this complication?','["A. Atherosclerosis of the aortic intima causing medial ischaemia","B. Cystic medial necrosis — accumulation of mucoid matrix and loss of elastic fibres and smooth muscle cells in the aortic media","C. Fibrinoid necrosis of vasa vasorum from immune complex deposition","D. Syphilitic aortitis destroying the adventitia"]','B','Marfan syndrome is caused by mutations in FBN1 (fibrillin-1), which is a structural component of elastic fibres and is essential for elastic fibre assembly and TGF-β regulation. Without normal fibrillin-1, the aortic media shows cystic medial necrosis (CMN): accumulation of mucoid (myxoid) extracellular matrix in pools, loss of smooth muscle cells, and fragmentation of elastic fibres. This weakens the aortic media, predisposing to progressive aortic root dilation and dissection.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Aortic Diseases (aneurysm, dissection)','easy','mcq','A Type A aortic dissection involves which portion of the aorta?','["A. Descending thoracic aorta distal to the left subclavian artery only","B. The ascending aorta (±aortic arch), regardless of where the intimal tear originates","C. The infrarenal abdominal aorta exclusively","D. The aortic arch only, between the brachiocephalic and left subclavian arteries"]','B','Aortic dissections are classified by the Stanford system: Type A = involves the ascending aorta (regardless of whether the descending aorta is also involved); Type B = involves only the descending aorta (distal to the left subclavian). Type A is a surgical emergency because the dissection can rupture into the pericardium (tamponade), obstruct coronary ostia (MI), disrupt the aortic valve (acute AR), or extend into the carotid arteries (stroke). Type B can often be managed medically.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Aortic Diseases (aneurysm, dissection)','medium','mcq','A 65-year-old hypertensive man presents with sudden-onset tearing chest pain radiating to the back. CT aortogram shows a false lumen in the descending thoracic aorta with an intimal flap. The ascending aorta is not involved. What is the most likely location of the intimal tear?','["A. At the aortic root, just above the coronary ostia","B. At the aortic isthmus, just distal to the origin of the left subclavian artery","C. At the diaphragmatic hiatus","D. At the origin of the coeliac axis"]','B','In Type B aortic dissection, the intimal tear most commonly occurs at the aortic isthmus — just distal to the left subclavian artery at the site of the ligamentum arteriosum. This is a point of anatomical fixation where haemodynamic stress concentrates. In Type A dissection, the tear most commonly occurs in the ascending aorta within a few centimetres of the aortic valve. Hypertension is the most important risk factor for dissection in adults >40 years.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Aortic Diseases (aneurysm, dissection)','medium','mcq','Syphilitic aortitis (tertiary syphilis) preferentially affects the thoracic aorta and can cause aortic aneurysm. What is the pathological mechanism?','["A. Direct spirochaetal invasion of aortic smooth muscle cells causing medial necrosis","B. Treponema pallidum-induced endarteritis obliterans of vasa vasorum, causing medial ischaemia, loss of elastic tissue, and saccular aneurysm formation","C. Type III hypersensitivity reaction causing immune complex deposition in the aortic adventitia","D. Atherosclerosis accelerated by syphilitic endothelial injury"]','B','In tertiary syphilis, Treponema pallidum invades the adventitia of the aorta, triggering an inflammatory obliterative endarteritis of the vasa vasorum. This destroys the blood supply to the aortic media, causing medial ischaemia with loss of smooth muscle cells and elastic fibres. The weakened media allows aneurysm formation — characteristically saccular, thoracic (ascending and arch), often involving the coronary ostia and aortic valve annulus. Coronary ostial stenosis, aortic regurgitation, and aneurysm rupture are sequelae.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Aortic Diseases (aneurysm, dissection)','medium','mcq','An AAA contains a large laminated mural thrombus. Which of the following correctly describes the haemodynamic consequence of this thrombus?','["A. The thrombus prevents further aneurysm dilation by reinforcing the wall","B. The thrombus does not protect against rupture and can serve as a source of distal thromboemboli","C. The thrombus always calcifies and is self-limiting","D. The thrombus blocks the renal artery ostia and causes acute renal failure"]','B','Laminated mural thrombus within an AAA is common (present in most large AAAs). Despite being present, the thrombus does not protect against rupture — the outer wall of the aneurysm remains exposed to full systemic pressure. The thrombus itself is a source of peripheral thromboemboli (causing blue toe syndrome, digital ischaemia). The thrombus can also occasionally occlude the origins of mesenteric or renal arteries as it extends to those ostia.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Aortic Diseases (aneurysm, dissection)','medium','mcq','A pathologist examining a thoracic aortic aneurysm notes that it is saccular in shape and involves the ascending aorta with "tree-bark" intimal wrinkling. Which aetiology is most consistent with these gross findings?','["A. Atherosclerosis","B. Syphilitic (luetic) aortitis","C. Marfan syndrome with cystic medial necrosis","D. Ehlers-Danlos syndrome"]','B','The "tree-bark" or wrinkled intimal appearance of the thoracic aorta is the classic gross finding of syphilitic aortitis, caused by scarring and wrinkling of the intima overlying areas of medial destruction. Atherosclerotic aneurysms are typically fusiform and infrarenal. Marfan aneurysms are fusiform, involve the aortic root/ascending aorta, and do not have tree-bark appearance. Saccular aneurysms of the thoracic ascending aorta with tree-bark intima = think syphilis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Aortic Diseases (aneurysm, dissection)','hard','mcq','A patient with aortic dissection has a proximal tear with the false lumen compressing the true lumen. Which of the following is the most dangerous immediate consequence of the false lumen propagating retrograde toward the heart?','["A. Occlusion of the coeliac axis causing bowel ischaemia","B. Compression of the true aortic lumen causing lower limb ischaemia","C. Haemopericardium causing cardiac tamponade or shearing of the coronary ostia causing acute MI","D. Dissection extending into renal arteries causing acute renal failure"]','C','In Type A dissection, the most immediately life-threatening complications arise from retrograde propagation of the false lumen toward the aortic root: (1) rupture into the pericardial sac (haemopericardium, cardiac tamponade — rapid death), (2) shearing of the coronary artery ostia (acute MI — most commonly RCA, causing inferior MI), and (3) disruption of the aortic valve annulus (acute severe aortic regurgitation with acute LV failure). These proximal complications explain why Type A is a surgical emergency.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Aortic Diseases (aneurysm, dissection)','hard','mcq','Research into the pathogenesis of AAA shows that MMP-9 and MMP-12 levels are markedly elevated in AAA tissue compared to normal aorta. Which cell type is the primary source of these enzymes in AAA?','["A. Vascular smooth muscle cells in the media","B. Macrophages (and neutrophils) recruited by atherosclerosis-related inflammation in the adventitia and media","C. Endothelial cells responding to turbulent flow","D. Mast cells releasing stored proteases"]','B','AAA formation involves proteolytic destruction of aortic medial matrix (elastin, collagen) by matrix metalloproteinases. The primary cellular sources are macrophages and neutrophils recruited into the aortic wall as part of the atherosclerosis-related inflammatory process. MMP-9 (gelatinase B) and MMP-12 (macrophage elastase) degrade elastin and collagen, weakening the media. This inflammation-driven matrix destruction — combined with loss of SMCs (possibly through macrophage-induced apoptosis) — creates the structural weakness enabling aneurysm expansion.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Aortic Diseases (aneurysm, dissection)','medium','mcq','Which of the following correctly describes an inflammatory aortic aneurysm, a variant of AAA?','["A. An aneurysm with fibrinoid necrosis from systemic vasculitis affecting the aortic wall","B. An AAA with dense inflammatory infiltrate in the wall and adherent perianeurysmal fibrosis encasing adjacent structures","C. A mycotic aneurysm with suppurative inflammation and bacterial colonies","D. An aneurysm complicating giant cell arteritis of the thoracic aorta"]','B','Inflammatory AAA accounts for 5–10% of all AAAs. They are characterised by a grossly thickened aortic wall with dense periadventitial fibrous tissue (desmoplastic reaction) that can encase the duodenum, ureter, and inferior vena cava. Microscopically, there is a dense inflammatory infiltrate (lymphocytes, plasma cells, macrophages). Patients often have elevated ESR/CRP and may present with abdominal pain and weight loss. The inflammatory component is distinct from atherosclerosis; some cases may represent an autoimmune process.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Vasculitis & Small Vessel Disease','easy','mcq','A 70-year-old woman presents with a new headache, jaw claudication, and scalp tenderness over the temporal region. ESR is 95 mm/hr. Temporal artery biopsy shows granulomatous inflammation with giant cells and fragmentation of the internal elastic lamina. What is the most feared complication if untreated?','["A. Renal failure from glomerulonephritis","B. Permanent visual loss from ischaemic optic neuropathy","C. Aortic dissection","D. Pulmonary haemorrhage"]','B','Giant cell (temporal) arteritis (GCA) is the most common vasculitis in adults >50 years. It affects large and medium-sized arteries, particularly the cranial branches of the aortic arch. The most feared complication is blindness from ischaemic optic neuropathy (due to inflammation of the posterior ciliary or ophthalmic arteries — branches of the internal carotid). Visual loss may be sudden, permanent, and bilateral if treatment is delayed. Corticosteroids must be started immediately upon clinical suspicion without waiting for biopsy results.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Vasculitis & Small Vessel Disease','easy','mcq','Which vasculitis is characterised by the triad of upper respiratory tract granulomas, necrotising glomerulonephritis, and small-to-medium vessel vasculitis, and is associated with c-ANCA (PR3-ANCA)?','["A. Polyarteritis nodosa","B. Granulomatosis with polyangiitis (Wegener granulomatosis)","C. Microscopic polyangiitis","D. Churg-Strauss syndrome"]','B','Granulomatosis with polyangiitis (GPA, formerly Wegener granulomatosis) classically presents with the triad: (1) necrotising granulomatous inflammation of the upper (sinusitis, nasal ulceration, saddle-nose deformity) and lower respiratory tracts (pulmonary nodules/cavities), (2) necrotising vasculitis of small-to-medium vessels, and (3) focal necrotising glomerulonephritis (pauci-immune, ANCA-mediated). c-ANCA (PR3-ANCA) is highly specific (~95%) for GPA. Treatment with cyclophosphamide and rituximab is effective.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Vasculitis & Small Vessel Disease','easy','mcq','A 30-year-old Asian woman develops reduced pulses in both arms, hypertension, and a bruit over the left subclavian artery. Angiography shows irregular narrowing and occlusion of the aortic arch vessels. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["A. Giant cell arteritis","B. Takayasu arteritis","C. Polyarteritis nodosa","D. Kawasaki disease"]','B','Takayasu arteritis (''pulseless disease'') is a granulomatous vasculitis affecting the aorta and its major branches, predominantly in young Asian women. It causes transmural fibrous thickening of the aortic arch and great vessels, producing luminal narrowing and occlusion. Clinical features include weak/absent upper extremity pulses, blood pressure discrepancy between arms, claudication, ocular disturbances (hypertensive retinopathy), and constitutional symptoms. Histology shows granulomatous inflammation, similar to GCA but in younger patients.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Vasculitis & Small Vessel Disease','medium','mcq','A 45-year-old man presents with fever, weight loss, mononeuritis multiplex, hypertension, and mesenteric ischaemia. Angiography shows multiple microaneurysms in medium-sized arteries. Biopsy of an affected vessel shows transmural necrotising inflammation without granuloma formation, ANCA negative. What is the diagnosis?','["A. Granulomatosis with polyangiitis","B. Polyarteritis nodosa (PAN)","C. Churg-Strauss syndrome","D. Microscopic polyangiitis"]','B','Polyarteritis nodosa (PAN) is a necrotising vasculitis of medium-sized muscular arteries — it does NOT affect capillaries, venules, or arterioles (distinguishing it from microscopic polyangiitis). Lesions are segmental with skip areas, producing microaneurysms on angiography. Histology shows transmural fibrinoid necrosis and neutrophilic infiltrate (acute phase) or mononuclear infiltrate (healing phase). ANCA is typically negative. Renal arteries and mesenteric arteries are commonly affected; glomeruli are spared (only vessels, not capillaries).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Vasculitis & Small Vessel Disease','medium','mcq','A 4-year-old child presents with high fever for 5 days, bilateral conjunctival injection, strawberry tongue, cervical lymphadenopathy, and desquamation of the fingertips. Echocardiography shows dilation of the left anterior descending coronary artery (aneurysm). What is the diagnosis and first-line treatment?','["A. Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome — treat with antibiotics","B. Kawasaki disease — treat with IVIG and aspirin","C. Measles with cardiac complications — treat with vitamin A","D. Systemic juvenile idiopathic arthritis — treat with methotrexate"]','B','Kawasaki disease (mucocutaneous lymph node syndrome) is the most common cause of acquired heart disease in children in developed countries. It is a medium-vessel vasculitis causing coronary artery aneurysms in ~20% of untreated cases (which can rupture or thrombose, causing MI). The five principal features: fever ≥5 days, bilateral non-purulent conjunctivitis, oral/pharyngeal erythema/strawberry tongue, rash (polymorphous), cervical lymphadenopathy. IVIG + high-dose aspirin given early dramatically reduces coronary aneurysm risk.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Vasculitis & Small Vessel Disease','medium','mcq','A 35-year-old asthmatic develops peripheral eosinophilia, eosinophilic pneumonia, palpable purpura, and mononeuritis multiplex. p-ANCA (MPO-ANCA) is positive. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["A. Granulomatosis with polyangiitis","B. Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis (Churg-Strauss syndrome)","C. Microscopic polyangiitis","D. Polyarteritis nodosa with incidental asthma"]','B','Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis (EGPA, Churg-Strauss syndrome) is a small-to-medium vessel vasculitis characterised by three phases: (1) prodromal allergic disease (asthma, rhinitis), (2) peripheral blood and tissue eosinophilia, and (3) necrotising vasculitis with extravascular granulomas. The combination of asthma + eosinophilia + vasculitis is highly suggestive. p-ANCA (MPO-ANCA) is positive in ~40–60%. Cardiac involvement (eosinophilic myocarditis) is a leading cause of death.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Vasculitis & Small Vessel Disease','medium','mcq','A young male smoker develops ischaemia and ulceration of the fingers and toes with a history of Raynaud phenomenon and superficial thrombophlebitis. Angiography shows segmental occlusions of small and medium arteries with "corkscrew" collateral vessels. Pathology shows segmental transmural inflammation with thrombosis. What is the diagnosis?','["A. Atherosclerosis with distal embolisation","B. Thromboangiitis obliterans (Buerger disease)","C. Systemic lupus erythematosus vasculitis","D. Antiphospholipid antibody syndrome"]','B','Thromboangiitis obliterans (Buerger disease) is a segmental, thrombosing, inflammatory condition of small and medium arteries and veins of the extremities. It is almost exclusively seen in young male heavy smokers. The acute lesion shows a highly inflammatory thrombus with polymorphonuclear cells, microabscesses, and multinucleate giant cells within the thrombus (preserved internal elastic lamina distinguishes it from other vasculitides). Cessation of smoking is the only effective treatment.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Vasculitis & Small Vessel Disease','hard','mcq','A patient with ANCA-associated vasculitis (microscopic polyangiitis) shows p-ANCA (MPO-ANCA) positivity. What is the proposed mechanism by which MPO-ANCA activates neutrophils to cause vascular injury?','["A. MPO-ANCA activates complement C3, which coats vessel walls for neutrophil phagocytosis","B. MPO-ANCA binds to MPO expressed on the surface of activated or apoptotic neutrophils, activating them to release reactive oxygen species and proteolytic enzymes that damage vessel walls","C. MPO-ANCA forms immune complexes deposited in glomeruli, activating classical complement pathway","D. MPO-ANCA blocks the anti-inflammatory function of regulatory T cells"]','B','The ANCA pathogenesis model: (1) Drugs/infections induce ANCA formation against MPO (or PR3). (2) Proinflammatory stimuli cause neutrophils to upregulate MPO/PR3 on their surfaces. (3) ANCA binds surface-expressed antigen, activating neutrophils to produce a respiratory burst and release proteolytic enzymes. (4) These activated neutrophils adhere to and damage endothelial cells in small vessels. The "pauci-immune" glomerulonephritis (minimal immune deposits by immunofluorescence) is explained because the injury is neutrophil-mediated rather than immune complex-mediated.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Vasculitis & Small Vessel Disease','hard','mcq','A researcher compares the ANCA subtypes in different vasculitides. PR3-ANCA (c-ANCA) is associated with GPA, while MPO-ANCA (p-ANCA) is associated with MPA and EGPA. Which characteristic distinguishes the granulomas of GPA from those of GCA?','["A. GPA granulomas are intravascular; GCA granulomas are extravascular","B. GPA granulomas are large, span between vessels, and are associated with geographic areas of necrosis — whereas GCA granulomas are within the vessel wall and associated with the internal elastic lamina","C. GPA granulomas contain Langerhans giant cells; GCA granulomas contain Touton giant cells","D. GPA granulomas are non-caseating; GCA granulomas show central caseating necrosis"]','B','GPA granulomas are ''irregular'' or geographic necrotising granulomas that span between vessels and are associated with areas of tissue necrosis — they are large and destructive. GCA granulomas are confined within the vessel wall (particularly concentrated around the internal elastic lamina which is fragmented by the granulomatous inflammation), not extending into surrounding tissue. Both contain multinucleate giant cells but the giant cells in GCA surround and engulf fragments of the internal elastic lamina.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Vasculitis & Small Vessel Disease','medium','mcq','A patient with longstanding Raynaud phenomenon develops digital ulcers and is found to have telangiectasias, sclerodactyly, and anti-Scl-70 antibodies. Biopsy of a digital artery shows intimal proliferation and fibrosis. What is the underlying pathogenesis of the vascular lesion in systemic sclerosis?','["A. Immune complex deposition causing type III hypersensitivity vasculitis","B. Endothelial cell injury (possibly immune-mediated) causing repeated cycles of ischaemia, reactive intimal fibrosis, and progressive luminal obliteration","C. Neutrophilic infiltration causing fibrinoid necrosis similar to PAN","D. Eosinophilic granulomas causing obliterative endarteritis"]','B','In systemic sclerosis (scleroderma), vascular disease is a primary feature. Repeated endothelial injury (mechanism unclear — possibly anti-endothelial antibodies, T-cell mediated) triggers reactive intimal proliferation (intimal hyperplasia) and fibrosis of small arteries and arterioles. This progressive obliterative vasculopathy (without true vasculitis — minimal inflammation) reduces luminal diameter, causing chronic ischaemia. Raynaud phenomenon (episodic vasospasm) is the functional manifestation; digital ulcers/infarcts represent the structural disease.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Ischemic Heart Disease (Angina, MI, Sudden Cardiac Death)','medium','mcq','A 68-year-old man with a known history of ischaemic heart disease develops worsening exertional dyspnoea and mild angina without any acute MI. Echocardiogram shows multiple areas of hypokinesis with reduced global LV function. Coronary angiography shows severe multi-vessel disease. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["A. Dilated cardiomyopathy from viral aetiology","B. Chronic ischaemic heart disease (ischaemic cardiomyopathy) from recurrent ischaemia and cumulative myocyte loss","C. Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy with LVOTO","D. Takotsubo (stress) cardiomyopathy"]','B','Chronic ischaemic heart disease (chronic IHD / ischaemic cardiomyopathy) results from progressive cumulative myocardial damage — not from a single large MI, but from repeated episodes of ischaemia, hibernating myocardium, and microinfarcts in the setting of severe multi-vessel coronary artery disease. The result is patchy replacement fibrosis interspersed with viable but dysfunctional myocardium. The heart appears enlarged and pale with areas of scarring. Clinically it presents as heart failure with reduced ejection fraction in a patient with known CAD.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Atherosclerosis & Arterial Disease','medium','mcq','A 55-year-old man has elevated C-reactive protein (CRP) despite normal LDL levels. His cardiologist considers this an additional cardiovascular risk factor. What is the pathophysiological role of systemic inflammation in atherogenesis?','["A. CRP directly deposits in vessel walls, forming the fibrous cap","B. Inflammation promotes endothelial dysfunction, monocyte recruitment, foam cell formation, and plaque instability — CRP is a surrogate marker of this process","C. Elevated CRP stimulates the liver to produce more LDL","D. Systemic inflammation has no proven role in atherosclerosis"]','B','Inflammation plays a central role at every stage of atherogenesis — from early endothelial activation and monocyte recruitment (fatty streak formation) through foam cell accumulation, fibrous cap formation, and ultimately plaque rupture. C-reactive protein (CRP) is a hepatic acute-phase reactant induced by IL-6 from inflammatory cells within plaques and systemically. High-sensitivity CRP (hsCRP) independently predicts cardiovascular events and is used clinically for risk stratification, particularly in intermediate-risk individuals (JUPITER trial: rosuvastatin reduced events in high-CRP, normal-LDL patients).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
;